1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
Okay, welcome to chapter 11,

2
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:04,260
dealing with the exocrine pancreas, gallbladder, and liver.

3
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:07,160
We're going to begin with the exocrine pancreas.

4
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:10,000
Now remember that the pancreas is sort of two organs in one.

5
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:13,860
There's an exocrine pancreas that's going to produce the enzymes

6
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:16,240
necessary for digestion,

7
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:19,120
while there's also an endocrine pancreas,

8
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:21,240
which would secrete hormones into the blood.

9
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:22,860
In this particular chapter,

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:25,840
we're only going to discuss the exocrine pancreas,

11
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,880
and we'll leave the endocrine pancreas for the endocrine chapter.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,180
The first disorder is annular pancreas.

13
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:33,940
This is a developmental malformation.

14
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,020
Here, the pancreas forms a ring around the duodenum,

15
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:39,980
leading to a risk of duodenal obstruction.

16
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:43,240
It's a fairly high-yield topic to show up on examinations

17
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:46,280
because of the fact that it is a good tie-in with embryology.

18
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:48,660
Now let's go back to the normal and remind ourselves

19
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:52,500
that the pancreas sits here in this sort of C-shaped bend

20
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:54,420
of the proximal duodenum,

21
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:57,880
and it's slightly posterior to the duodenum.

22
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,620
Now imagine if the pancreas were to be shifted forward

23
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:05,560
and then were to turn on itself and to wrap around the duodenum,

24
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:07,960
then this would form a ring around the duodenum,

25
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:10,580
which could potentially lead to duodenal obstruction,

26
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,720
and that's called an annular pancreas,

27
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:15,080
when sort of the head of the pancreas bends around

28
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:16,880
and wraps around the duodenum.

29
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:19,860
Acute pancreatitis is when you get inflammation

30
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:21,080
and hemorrhage of the pancreas.

31
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:24,500
Now basically, the issue here is that you get autodigestion

32
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:26,700
of the pancreas by the pancreatic enzymes.

33
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:29,520
So really, it's a story of premature activation

34
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:30,740
of the pancreatic enzymes.

35
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:33,820
Now recall that the pancreas produces digestive enzymes,

36
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:36,820
and those enzymes are produced in an inactive form.

37
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,620
It's not until they get into the duodenum

38
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:41,360
that the activation actually occurs.

39
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:43,900
However, if there was a premature activation

40
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,120
within the pancreas,

41
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:47,880
then we would have autodigestion of the pancreas,

42
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,900
and that's called acute pancreatitis.

43
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:51,520
Now it's very high yield to note,

44
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:53,040
for the purposes of examinations,

45
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:55,960
that the first enzyme that classically would get activated

46
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:56,760
would be trypsin,

47
00:01:57,040 --> 00:01:58,920
and then trypsin would subsequently activate

48
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:00,000
all the other enzymes.

49
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:02,380
When patients have acute pancreatitis,

50
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:04,680
they get autodigestion of the pancreas,

51
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:07,080
and this results in a liquefactive necrosis

52
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:09,000
of the pancreas with extensive hemorrhage,

53
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:11,120
a hemorrhage because there's a lot of blood vessels

54
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:12,100
within the pancreas.

55
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:14,520
So we're going to get a liquefactive necrosis

56
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:15,600
of the pancreas itself.

57
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:18,600
At the same time, remember that surrounding the pancreas

58
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:19,540
is adipose tissue,

59
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:22,100
and these digestive enzymes would also digest

60
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:24,300
the adipose tissue, releasing the fatty acids.

61
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:26,960
The release of fatty acids would then undergo saponification,

62
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:29,360
and that is called fat necrosis.

63
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:32,500
And so you get two types of necrosis in pancreatitis.

64
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:35,480
You get liquefactive necrosis of the pancreas itself,

65
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,700
and then fat necrosis of the peripancreatic fat.

66
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:41,320
The two most common causes of acute pancreatitis

67
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:42,900
are alcohol and gallstones.

68
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:46,480
The idea here is that alcohol creates contraction

69
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:49,880
at the sphincter of adi at the ampulla that enters into the duodenum.

70
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:51,780
Now recall that the pancreatic enzymes,

71
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:53,940
they drain via the pancreatic duct,

72
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,340
eventually entering the ampulla,

73
00:02:55,660 --> 00:02:57,060
and then going into the duodenum.

74
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,500
If there's contraction of the sphincter of adi here,

75
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,920
this would decrease the drainage of the pancreas,

76
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:04,980
increasing the risk of premature activation of the enzymes.

77
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:07,580
An additional common cause is gallstones.

78
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:10,780
Again, if you have a gallstone that blocks here at the ampulla,

79
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,460
this would block the ability of the pancreas to drain.

80
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:16,100
And of course, a decreased drainage of the pancreas

81
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:19,200
would increase the risk for premature activation of the enzymes.

82
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,340
Other causes of acute pancreatitis include trauma.

83
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:25,620
One of the high-yield examples of trauma to be aware of

84
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:28,360
would be automobile accidents in children.

85
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:30,980
Now imagine if a child is wearing their seatbelt

86
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:34,040
such that it overlies the abdomen instead of the pelvis.

87
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:35,820
Then were an accident to occur,

88
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:37,880
the seatbelt would actually pull on the abdomen,

89
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:40,840
and compressing the abdominal structures, including the pancreas.

90
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:44,200
This could then lead to premature activation of pancreatic enzymes.

91
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:46,400
Hypercalcemia and hyperlipidemia

92
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,980
are also important causes of acute pancreatitis.

93
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:51,340
The way by which I remember hypercalcemia

94
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:54,020
is to just remember that calcium is an activator of enzymes.

95
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:56,180
And so if there's a high calcium within the blood,

96
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:58,860
obviously that high calcium could potentially activate

97
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:00,380
the pancreatic enzymes.

98
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:03,220
Drugs can also cause acute pancreatitis.

99
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:06,820
The venom from a scorpion can also cause acute pancreatitis.

100
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:10,580
Recall that the mumps virus has a propensity to infect the pancreas,

101
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,940
increasing the risk for acute pancreatitis.

102
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:15,920
And finally, rupture of a posterior duodenal ulcer

103
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:17,640
can result in pancreatitis.

104
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,580
And that's because the head of the pancreas

105
00:04:19,580 --> 00:04:22,360
sits slightly posterior to the duodenum.

106
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:25,300
When a patient presents with acute pancreatitis,

107
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:27,780
they classically have epigastric abdominal pain

108
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:28,920
that radiates to the back.

109
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:30,960
In addition, they'll have nausea and vomiting.

110
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:33,780
There can be peri-umbilical and flank hemorrhage.

111
00:04:33,780 --> 00:04:36,440
The idea here is that the necrosis can spread

112
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:38,180
into the peri-umbilical soft tissues

113
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:39,340
and the retroperitoneum,

114
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:41,720
giving this peri-umbilical and flank hemorrhage

115
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:43,720
as you get destruction of the blood vessels

116
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:44,820
in this region as well.

117
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:47,660
Laboratory examination would show an elevation

118
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:49,260
of serum lipase and amylase.

119
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:50,900
Again, both of these are enzymes

120
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:52,080
that come from the pancreas.

121
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:54,120
But it's important to note that lipase

122
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:56,600
is more specific for pancreatic damage.

123
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:58,820
And that's because amylase could also be seen

124
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:00,860
if there was damage to the salivary gland.

125
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:04,180
Finally, on laboratory exam, there could be hypocalcemia.

126
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:07,460
Now, the idea here is that when you have acute pancreatitis,

127
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:10,140
you get liquefactive necrosis of the pancreas,

128
00:05:10,420 --> 00:05:13,040
but fat necrosis of the peripancreatic fat.

129
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:15,680
And that fat necrosis would lead to saponification,

130
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:18,080
and saponification would use up calcium

131
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:20,460
as calcium was deposited within the fat.

132
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:23,400
So this eventually could result in a hypocalcemia.

133
00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:27,020
In fact, having hypocalcemia is a poor prognostic indicator

134
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:28,780
because of the fact that it indicates

135
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:30,720
that there's such severe necrosis

136
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:33,900
that there's also necrosis of the peripancreatic fat.

137
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:36,860
Complications of pancreatitis include shock.

138
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:40,400
The idea here is that not only are you digesting the pancreas,

139
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:42,940
but you're digesting the myriad of blood vessels

140
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:44,280
present within the pancreas.

141
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:47,860
And so that extensive hemorrhage could result in shock.

142
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:50,160
In addition, there's going to be fluid sequestration

143
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:51,480
from all of the tissue damage.

144
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:54,640
A pancreatic pseudocyst is also a complication.

145
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:57,060
In this particular case, you get fibrous tissue

146
00:05:57,060 --> 00:06:00,200
that surrounds liquefactive necrosis and pancreatic enzymes.

147
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,120
So it's called a pseudocyst

148
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:03,520
because it doesn't have a true lining.

149
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:05,220
Instead, it's just fibrous tissue

150
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:08,600
that walls off pancreatic enzymes and liquefactive necrosis.

151
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:11,780
Now, these patients would present with acute pancreatitis,

152
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:14,880
which eventually led to the progression of an abdominal mass

153
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:17,560
and with persistence of the amylase enzyme.

154
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:20,540
Now, of course, when the patient has acute pancreatitis,

155
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:21,940
the amylase would go up,

156
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:24,100
and then we would expect the amylase to go down

157
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:27,040
as the bout of acute pancreatitis comes to a close.

158
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:28,740
However, in this particular case,

159
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:31,100
there would be persistence of the amylase enzyme.

160
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:34,480
The further complication of a pancreatic pseudocyst

161
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:35,360
is that it can rupture.

162
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:36,460
And when it ruptures,

163
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:38,220
you get release of all of those enzymes

164
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:39,940
into the abdominal cavity,

165
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:41,960
which then results in extensive hemorrhage

166
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:43,120
within the abdominal cavity.

167
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:44,960
Patients with acute pancreatitis

168
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:46,920
can also develop a pancreatic abscess.

169
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:48,620
This is usually due to E. coli,

170
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:51,160
and it presents with abdominal pain, high fever,

171
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:53,280
and a persistently elevated amylase

172
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,700
in the face of a recent bout of acute pancreatitis.

173
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,760
Finally, important complications are DIC and ARDS.

174
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:01,240
Now, the way I remember this

175
00:07:01,240 --> 00:07:02,360
is that the pancreatic enzymes

176
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:03,760
can potentially get into the blood,

177
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:05,700
and so they can chew on the coag factors,

178
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:07,660
activating them, giving the patient DIC.

179
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:08,920
At the same time,

180
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:10,140
the enzymes, when they're in the blood,

181
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:12,480
can chew on the alveolar-capillary interface,

182
00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:14,500
damaging the alveolar-capillary interface,

183
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:16,080
giving the patient ARDS,

184
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:18,840
or acute respiratory distress syndrome.

185
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:21,160
Chronic pancreatitis is usually due

186
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:23,600
to recurrent bouts of acute pancreatitis,

187
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:24,640
and so you can imagine,

188
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:26,800
as you keep getting bouts of acute pancreatitis,

189
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:28,620
eventually you're going to get fibrosis

190
00:07:28,620 --> 00:07:29,840
of the pancreatic parenchyma,

191
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:32,120
and that is called chronic pancreatitis.

192
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:34,960
Now, the most common causes are alcohol in adults

193
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:36,700
and cystic fibrosis in children.

194
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:38,740
If you go back to your original list

195
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,120
of causes of pancreatitis,

196
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:41,760
you'll see that the vast majority of them

197
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:43,860
would only occur once in a patient's lifetime.

198
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:45,740
For example, if the patient had a drug

199
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:47,080
that caused acute pancreatitis,

200
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:47,980
you'd remove the drug.

201
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:49,880
Obviously, a patient wouldn't get bit

202
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,000
by scorpions more than once, for example.

203
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:53,680
Additionally, trauma would only occur

204
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:54,920
rarely in a patient's life.

205
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:56,460
However, alcohol is something

206
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:58,620
that patients have a difficult time giving up,

207
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:00,880
and so multiple bouts of acute pancreatitis

208
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:03,320
with alcohol can result in chronic pancreatitis.

209
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:05,200
In the case of cystic fibrosis,

210
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,280
there are thick secretions coming from the pancreas.

211
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:09,780
This slows the ability of the pancreas to drain,

212
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:13,580
and hence, increasing the risk for early activation

213
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:16,400
or premature activation of the pancreatic enzymes.

214
00:08:16,840 --> 00:08:18,520
It's important to note that many cases

215
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:20,400
of chronic pancreatitis are idiopathic,

216
00:08:20,460 --> 00:08:21,940
and we don't exactly know the cause.

217
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:24,580
The clinical features of chronic pancreatitis

218
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:26,000
are epigastric abdominal pain

219
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,120
that radiates to the back,

220
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:29,880
but in addition, there'll be pancreatic insufficiency.

221
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:31,900
Now, there are a couple of derivative points

222
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:35,120
that arise from this notion of pancreatic insufficiency.

223
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:37,520
Of course, if you damage the pancreatic parenchyma,

224
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:38,900
you're not going to be able to produce

225
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:41,100
the enzymes that the pancreas would normally make.

226
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:42,980
Because you can't produce the enzymes,

227
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:44,240
the first complication will be

228
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:46,780
that the patients will get malabsorption with steatorrhea.

229
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:49,900
Of course, you need the enzymes to assist in digestion,

230
00:08:50,380 --> 00:08:52,700
and so no enzymes is going to lead to malabsorption

231
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:55,920
with increased fat within the stool or steatorrhea.

232
00:08:56,920 --> 00:08:58,780
At the same time, the patients will also develop

233
00:08:58,780 --> 00:09:00,340
fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies

234
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:02,320
because if there's poor digestion of fat,

235
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:05,040
you won't be able to absorb the fat-soluble vitamins

236
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:06,800
A, E, D, and K.

237
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:09,060
Now, one more derivative principle

238
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:10,440
from pancreatic insufficiency,

239
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,760
it's important to note that amylase and lipase,

240
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:14,700
which were good markers of acute pancreatitis,

241
00:09:15,020 --> 00:09:16,680
are not useful for chronic pancreatitis,

242
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:18,660
and that's because the vast majority of the pancreas

243
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:19,440
has been destroyed,

244
00:09:19,740 --> 00:09:21,840
and so you're not making a lot of amylase and lipase

245
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:22,480
to begin with.

246
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:25,000
An additional feature of chronic pancreatitis

247
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,980
will be that you'll have dystrophic calcifications

248
00:09:26,980 --> 00:09:27,800
of the pancreas.

249
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:29,820
On imaging, you'll see this calcification

250
00:09:29,820 --> 00:09:31,060
wrapping around the pancreas,

251
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:33,740
and often the pancreas can be massively calcified

252
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:35,340
when there's chronic pancreatitis.

253
00:09:35,340 --> 00:09:37,700
If there's extensive damage to the pancreas,

254
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:39,760
you'll eventually damage the islets,

255
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:41,280
and when there's damage to the islets,

256
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:44,040
the patient can present with secondary diabetes mellitus.

257
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:46,060
However, this is a late complication.

258
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:49,040
And finally, patients with chronic pancreatitis

259
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:51,280
have an increased risk for pancreatic carcinoma.

260
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:54,240
Pancreatic carcinoma is an adenocarcinoma

261
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:56,040
that arise from the pancreatic ducts.

262
00:09:56,300 --> 00:09:57,980
Now, remember, when you go back to your histology,

263
00:09:58,100 --> 00:09:59,360
the pancreas, you have ducts

264
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:00,860
that are carrying the secretions,

265
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:02,160
and then you have acini,

266
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:03,920
which actually make the enzymes.

267
00:10:04,380 --> 00:10:06,320
And so, this is an adenocarcinoma

268
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:08,220
that arise from the pancreatic ducts.

269
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:10,020
It's most commonly seen in the elderly.

270
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:11,400
The average age is 70,

271
00:10:11,740 --> 00:10:13,240
and there are two major risk factors

272
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:14,080
that you have to know.

273
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:15,140
Number one, smoking,

274
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:17,320
and number two, chronic pancreatitis,

275
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:19,200
as I just mentioned on the prior slide.

276
00:10:19,860 --> 00:10:21,420
Patients with pancreatic carcinoma

277
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:23,080
generally present late in the course.

278
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:24,700
However, when they do present,

279
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:27,860
they classically have epigastric abdominal pain

280
00:10:27,860 --> 00:10:28,920
and weight loss.

281
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:31,160
Now, pancreatic carcinoma can arise

282
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:32,380
within the head of the pancreas

283
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:34,420
or the body or tail of the pancreas.

284
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:36,740
When it arises within the head of the pancreas,

285
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,260
the classic finding would be obstructive jaundice

286
00:10:39,260 --> 00:10:41,640
with pale stools and a palpable gallbladder.

287
00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:44,360
The idea here is that when you've got this tumor

288
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:45,520
in the head of the pancreas,

289
00:10:45,780 --> 00:10:48,060
you're going to block the flow of bile.

290
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:49,700
When you block the flow of bile,

291
00:10:49,780 --> 00:10:51,220
then whatever's present within the bile

292
00:10:51,220 --> 00:10:52,340
will leak out into the blood.

293
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:55,140
And so, because there's bilirubin in the bile,

294
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:57,820
bilirubin, in particular conjugated bilirubin,

295
00:10:57,820 --> 00:10:59,360
will now leak out into the blood,

296
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:01,020
giving the patient's jaundice.

297
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:03,200
In addition, because bile does not leak out

298
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:04,000
into the duodenum,

299
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,860
you will not get mixing of bile with the stool.

300
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:09,340
And hence, because the stool receives its color

301
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:11,220
from a derivative of the bilirubin,

302
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:13,440
the stools will become pale in color.

303
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:16,020
Finally, because there's blockage of this tract,

304
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:18,480
you may get back up pressure into the gallbladder,

305
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:20,240
which would expand the gallbladder,

306
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:21,220
making it palpable.

307
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:23,100
And so, these are some of the key findings,

308
00:11:23,180 --> 00:11:24,380
as I stated here in the slide.

309
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:30,880
Again, this would arise when tumors are present

310
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:32,140
in the head of the pancreas.

311
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:34,620
If a tumor is present in the body or the tail,

312
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:36,540
there's a possibility for that tumor

313
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:38,900
to knock out the islet cells of the pancreas,

314
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:41,620
which are predominantly present in the body or the tail.

315
00:11:41,940 --> 00:11:43,160
And so, those patients can present

316
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:44,820
with secondary diabetes mellitus.

317
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:47,040
Now, remember that pancreatic carcinoma

318
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:48,600
is a disease of the elderly.

319
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:51,600
And so, if you have a thin, elderly person

320
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:53,280
that presents with diabetes,

321
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:54,760
you should think about the fact

322
00:11:54,760 --> 00:11:55,960
that that's not the demographic

323
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:57,560
for a normal diabetic patient.

324
00:11:57,880 --> 00:12:00,140
Remember that type 2 diabetics are usually obese.

325
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:03,580
And type 2 diabetics normally present in middle age,

326
00:12:03,660 --> 00:12:05,500
for example, the age of 40 or 45.

327
00:12:06,020 --> 00:12:08,120
And so, if you have this thin, elderly female

328
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:09,000
on a board exam

329
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,940
that all of a sudden presents with diabetes,

330
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:12,860
think about pancreatic carcinoma

331
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:14,340
as an important possibility.

332
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:16,120
And that's particularly high yield

333
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:17,400
for examination purposes.

334
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:19,240
Additional clinical features

335
00:12:19,240 --> 00:12:20,360
of pancreatic carcinoma

336
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:22,000
is that it can lead to pancreatitis.

337
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,540
Of course, if the tumor blocks the pancreatic duct,

338
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:27,180
that would decrease the drainage of the enzymes,

339
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:29,700
increasing the risk for premature activation.

340
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:31,300
Patients can also develop

341
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:33,080
migratory thrombophlebitis.

342
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:35,380
This presents a swelling, erythema,

343
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:37,360
and tenderness in extremities.

344
00:12:37,660 --> 00:12:39,740
And it's seen in about 10% of patients.

345
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:41,860
And finally, it's important to note

346
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:43,020
that the serum tumor marker

347
00:12:43,020 --> 00:12:45,920
for pancreatic carcinoma is CA19-9.

348
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:47,980
Treatment of pancreatic carcinoma

349
00:12:47,980 --> 00:12:49,360
involves on-block removal

350
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:51,480
of the head and neck of the pancreas.

351
00:12:51,480 --> 00:12:53,180
The proximal duodenum

352
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:54,280
and the gallbladder.

353
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:55,820
So we can use our image here

354
00:12:55,820 --> 00:12:56,880
to highlight what would happen.

355
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:58,300
Again, you would be removing

356
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:00,580
the head and neck of the pancreas,

357
00:13:01,020 --> 00:13:01,840
the duodenum,

358
00:13:02,100 --> 00:13:02,900
and the gallbladder.

359
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:04,660
And that's called the Whipple procedure.

360
00:13:05,380 --> 00:13:06,700
Now, it's important to note, however,

361
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:08,240
that pancreatic carcinoma

362
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:09,760
has a very poor prognosis.

363
00:13:10,220 --> 00:13:10,660
And in fact,

364
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:11,600
the one-year survival

365
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:13,060
is less than 10%.

366
00:13:13,060 --> 00:13:14,820
That concludes our section

367
00:13:14,820 --> 00:13:15,840
on pancreas.

